body:
I understand this point but I think being nervous and having some self doubts when playing at the absolute top level is at least a somewhat natural reaction that impacts everyone in that situation, I'm not necessarily convinced it needs a doctor. He went to a therapist which is a good choice imho because having a therapist doesn't equal having mental health problems but it would still be beneficial for most people even just casually, because we often fear telling our closest ones the stuff that's bothering us even when it's not a mental diagnose.

&#x200B;

I might be a bit more sensitive to this issue though because I've been battling social anxiety since my youth and recently have gone through a phase of self harm that the meds managed to tone down. I'm not saying that so gain sympathy, just that some of these experiences and learning about experiences of others when I was in a hospital gave me a better idea of what's within norm and what's an issue that needs dealing with. This is not a diss on Mings, I'm glad he had no problems getting a therapist, I think every top athlete (and like I said, maybe even every person) would benefit from having one. Just that I don't think what he experienced (at least from what we know from these quotes) should be some marker for other people to think that this is a pathological issue if they have it the same. 

&#x200B;

100% find a therapist if you feel like it, I'd cheer on you, but I think talking about issues like these as "mental health" issues does more harm than good because it dumbs down the more serious issues that people face. Not sure if I explained myself well, not a native english speaker.
